WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:02.600
Text on an ivory background: "Every focus app has a skip button." The word "skip" is struck through.

00:02.600 --> 00:04.667
The CodeLock dashboard on Windows. A pointer clicks "15m" and a real 15-minute countdown starts. Caption: This one doesn't.

00:04.667 --> 00:06.133
Fifteen minutes later, labelled on screen. The countdown's last seconds, up close: 0:01.

00:06.133 --> 00:07.733
The lock takes over the whole screen with a programming problem, Two Sum. Caption: Time's up. The screen locks.

00:07.733 --> 00:09.433
A first attempt is typed into the editor, sped up eight times. Caption: Solve a coding problem to get back in.

00:09.433 --> 00:12.900
The pointer clicks Submit. An arrow points to the real verdict: Wrong answer, 2 of 5 tests passed. Caption: Wrong answer? Still locked.

00:12.900 --> 00:15.900
The fix is typed, sped up eight times, and submitted. Caption: Fix it. Submit again.

00:15.900 --> 00:18.100
The app's result, held as a still: Accepted, 5 of 5 tests passed, 100 ms within the 162 ms speed budget. Caption: Correct, and fast enough.

00:18.100 --> 00:20.300
The lock lets go and the picture settles back into the frame: "You solved Two Sum without a hint." Caption: Unlocked. Back to work.

00:20.300 --> 00:23.133
The lion mark. CodeLock. Earn your next break. See how it works at tommydeleon.com. Solo project, Windows desktop app, not released.
